{"chunk_id": "12b13d3b9270", "text": "In this section, Yayati explains to Ashtaka the ideals of different spiritual paths\u2014householder, forest-dweller (Vanaprastha), wandering mendicant, and silent sage (Muni). True virtue lies in charity, hospitality, restraint, and non\u2011injury. A genuine Bhikshu or Muni is defined less by location than by inner detachment: one may live in the woods or a village yet remain withdrawn from worldly desire. Through ascetic discipline, purity of food and conduct, and meditation, the sage becomes indifferent to pleasure and pain and ultimately unites with Brahman. When asked whether the ascetic or the knower reaches liberation first, Yayati declares that knowledge of the illusory nature of the world leads most directly to salvation, while yoga may take longer across lifetimes. The passage ends dramatically as Yayati reveals he has fallen from heaven after exhausting his merits and must descend toward hell, though he was granted the grace of meeting the wise before his fall.\nYayati explains paths of asceticism and knowledge, arguing that true knowledge leads to quicker liberation, before revealing he has fallen from heaven due to exhausted merit.\nThemes: ascetic ideals, knowledge vs yoga, detachment, karma and merit, fall from heaven", "vector": [0.011614006012678146, -0.012758476659655571, 0.003159145126119256, 0.0045880405232310295, -0.025422144681215286, 0.030744949355721474, -0.026911988854408264, -0.025286704301834106, -0.02083071880042553, -0.013022584840655327, 0.00974493008106947, 0.005509034264832735, 0.002046841662377119, 0.025814922526478767, 0.0008888270240277052, -0.013713330961763859, 0.04136347025632858, 0.0006556157022714615, 0.028198670595884323, -0.006521450821310282, -0.014573376625776291, 0.023349909111857414, -0.00557336863130331, -0.0051839775405824184, 0.00028950365958735347, 0.01786457560956478, 0.021670449525117874, -0.015643354505300522, 0.0015152384294196963, -0.01302935741841793, -0.011072244495153427